            <ArticleTitle>Medical treatment of patients with heart failure or left ventricular dysfunction undergoing percutaneous coronary intervention.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>313-25</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Management of ischemic patients with pre-existing or new-onset left ventricular (LV) dysfunction poses a special challenge in terms of the timing of percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) and appropriate adjunctive medications to optimize outcome while minimizing risk. In a systematic fashion, this review attempts to provide a management scheme for patients with heart failure or LV dysfunction that present with stable angina, ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction, or unstable angina/non-ST-segment elevation myocardial infarction. By addressing therapeutic approaches to acute or decompensated heart failure and timing of coronary angiography based on severity of ischemia, we provide evidence-based recommendations for medications to initiate before, during, and following PCI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
